{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act criminal-conviction eviction causes","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of or liable for assault or threats against the landlord, criminal activity, theft, or human trafficking. A three-day Notice to Quit is required and suit must be filed within two years.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; 2-year limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential rental premises in New Jersey, including tenants who permit a convicted or delinquent person to reside on the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling evictions","requirement":"A landlord of an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may evict only on court order for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, or rule or lease violations. Three months' notice is required for at-will or year-to-year tenancies and one month for month-to-month.","key_value":"3-month or 1-month notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction rules","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses: buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, subject to the Act's exceptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled are excepted.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction law violation penalties","requirement":"A landlord evicting for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months or pay the former tenant triple damages plus attorney fees. One who evicts to board up, demolish or retire a building must not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; up to $10,000","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who obtain eviction based on personal occupancy or on permanently boarding up, demolishing or retiring a building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Treble damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, altering lease terms, or refusing to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, complains in good faith to a government authority, joins a tenant organization, or refuses a retaliatory lease change.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all rental dwellings except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant protections in foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure is not grounds for eviction. A buyer who will occupy the property must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit, and a lease beyond 90 days must run until it expires. New owners must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of residential rental property after foreclosure.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental property in foreclosure"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law providing longer time periods or additional tenant protections.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"A tenant who is at least 62, permanently disabled, or a qualifying disabled veteran and lived in a building being converted at least one year before the conversion recording date may be protected from eviction for up to 40 years.","key_value":"40 years' protection","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled veterans who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date; income limits apply.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extends protected-tenancy eviction protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties (currently only Hudson County) who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties; currently only Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted or being converted in qualified counties (Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 et seq.","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on excessive rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent above any applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not subject to rent control, impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property subject to a rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or not under rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.3,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
